    "scope": "# Security audit: PR #275, sessions suites stop their tmux servers (A-515)\n\nBranch lane/session-20260923-5c-leak at head 4a7ba2d5; base dev\n29a79cfc. One audit per PR (CONTRIB.md).\n\nDiff under audit, `git diff 29a79cfc..4a7ba2d5`, two commits: b5421bd0\n(ops/conductor/tests/fakes/session_harness.py and test_sessions.py:\nthe exited-pane row binds only after the pane is gone; rows stop their\ntmux server at teardown) and 4a7ba2d5 (test_sessions.py: a row that no\ntmux server or pane outlives the world).\n\nQuestions: does a teardown signal or kill a process it does not own (a\npid outside the world, a server on another socket, kill-server on a\nsocket the row did not create)? Is the socket path the world's own and\nunder 108 bytes? Can a stale or reused pid be signalled? Does the new\nrow fail on a planted leak, or pass on silence? Does anything reach\noutside the world's roots or the stdlib? Each finding carries practical\nyes|no; yes names the failing invocation. Quotation pairs with\nlocations. Verdict on the wire.\n",
